+ *
+ * rflogd - the firewall log collector (2026-11-08, backlog fwlog-nflog-plan.md sections 2, 7, 8).
+ *
+ * Credit: this daemon is a client of the Linux kernel's nfnetlink_log, part of the netfilter project
+ * (https://www.netfilter.org/; kernel implementation net/netfilter/nfnetlink_log.c, GPL-2.0-only). It talks to
+ * it only through the kernel's public UAPI headers (linux/netfilter/nfnetlink_log.h); no upstream code is copied.
+ * The text of each line reproduces the format of the kernel's xt_LOG target (nf_log_ipv4 / nf_log_ipv6) so the
+ * Firewall page, the diag and anything reading the old syslog lines keep working.
+ *
+ * Why: a firewall LOG rule costs ~100-300 us of CPU per line (printk, klogd, syslogd, the JFFS2 write, serialized on
+ * the log-buffer lock); "Logged packets type: Accepted / Both" on a busy LAN saturated the router and starved DNS.
+ * Every firewall log rule rc writes now targets NFLOG while this daemon runs (shared/reaper_fwlog.h): the kernel
+ * hands it the first 128 bytes of each logged packet over netlink, batched, and it writes the line itself - about
+ * 2 us of kernel time and 5-8 us here per line, on its own CPU, off syslog, off the serial console.
+ *
+ *   - one netlink socket per group (40 stock, 41 Warden, 42 the closed blob, 43 the rules engine), so a burst in one
+ *     source cannot make the kernel drop another's batches (netlink drops per receiving socket);
+ *   - every message carries the instance's sequence number: a gap is counted exactly (lost), never silent;
+ *   - lines a rule's limiter suppressed (the NFLOG flood ceiling, or the fallback's 10/s) are counted from the
+ *     rules' packet counters (iptables-save -c, once a minute): log rule vs the rule right after it;
+ *   - RAM ring /tmp/reaper/fwlog/current.log (-> prev.log at ram_kb); when the long-term store lists the fwlog
+ *     dataset, the same bytes go to <store>/fwlog/YYYY-MM-DD.log through the no-follow store helpers, oldest days
+ *     removed past keep_days / keep_mb; a remote syslog target (rc passes log_ipaddr:log_port) gets each line by UDP;
+ *   - stats.json for the page, rwatch and the diag.
+ * It reads no nvram: rc writes fwlog.conf and decides whether it runs. Root for the binds, then every capability
+ * but CAP_NET_ADMIN and CAP_NET_RAW (the once-a-minute iptables-save) is dropped, before the first byte from the
+ * network is parsed. Every attribute and header is bounds-checked against its length.
+ */
